        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.6k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6">
        <h2 class="flex items-center gap-2 text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mt-0 mb-4">
          <svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" class="w-5 h-5 text-[#FF5A1F]" fill="none" viewBox="0 0 24 24" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2"><path stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" d="M4 6h16M4 12h16M4 18h7"></path></svg>
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#physics" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">1. Why Decibel Ratings Mislead</a>
          <a href="#how-we-tested" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">2. How We Measured Both Kinds of Noise</a>
          <a href="#what-makes-quiet" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">3. What Actually Makes a Treadmill Quiet</a>
          <a href="#picks" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">4. The 5 Quietest Treadmills We Tested</a>
          <a href="#mats" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">5. Mats: The Cheapest Big Win</a>
          <a href="#placement" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">6. Placement and Floor Type</a>
          <a href="#technique" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">7. Free Fixes: Technique and Timing</a>
          <a href="#pads" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">8. Are Walking Pads Any Quieter?</a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">9. Final Recommendations</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="flex items-start gap-2 text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium text-sm hover:underline">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The decibel figure on a treadmill listing measures the wrong thing.
          It records airborne sound beside the machine, and the complaint that
          arrives from the flat below is about impact travelling through the
          building. Those are different phenomena with different solutions, and
          confusing them is why people spend a thousand dollars on a quiet
          machine and still get a note under the door.
        </p>

        <div id="physics" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. Why Decibel Ratings Mislead</h2>
          <p>
            A treadmill makes two kinds of noise. The first is airborne: motor
            hum, belt friction, the whirr of rollers. It radiates into the room,
            and it is what a microphone placed a metre from the deck records.
            Walls, doors and distance attenuate it quickly, which is why your
            treadmill is barely audible from the next room.
          </p>
          <p>
            The second is structure-borne. Every footfall delivers an impulse
            into the deck. Some of that energy is absorbed by cushioning; the
            rest passes into the frame, through the feet of the machine, into
            the floor, and then through joists, walls and concrete. Building
            structures conduct low-frequency energy extremely efficiently, and
            almost nothing attenuates it over the distance between two floors.
            That is the rhythmic thud your neighbour hears, and no amount of
            motor quietness affects it.
          </p>
          <p>
            The practical consequence is that the two rankings can invert. In
            our test, the machine with the lowest airborne reading was not the
            one with the lowest reading downstairs, and a walking pad that was
            near-silent in the air performed poorly through the floor because it
            has almost no cushioning to absorb impact before it reaches the
            structure. If neighbours are your constraint, judge machines on
            frame mass and cushioning, and treat the published decibel number as
            close to irrelevant.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="how-we-tested" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. How We Measured Both Kinds of Noise</h2>
          <p>
            Airborne noise was recorded one metre from the console at 2, 3 and 6
            mph, with the console audio muted, on a hard floor with no mat. That
            gives the comparable figure most reviews quote, and the values in
            the cards below are the 3 mph readings.
          </p>
          <p>
            Impact noise was the more useful test. Each machine ran in a
            first-floor room of a two-storey timber-framed house with a
            microphone in the room directly beneath, positioned centrally.
            We recorded the same speeds, with and without a 6mm rubber mat, and
            compared the low-frequency energy between them. This is not a
            laboratory measurement and we would not publish absolute figures
            from it, but the relative ordering was consistent across repeated
            runs, and it is the ordering the picks below reflect.
          </p>
          <p>
            One finding is worth stating before the products: the difference the
            mat made was larger than the difference between the best and worst
            machines. If you buy nothing else from this page, buy the mat. Our
            standard test protocol for everything else — cushioning, motor,
            durability — is documented in our
            <a href="/treadmill-reviews-2026/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">testing methodology</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="what-makes-quiet" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. What Actually Makes a Treadmill Quiet</h2>
          <p>
            <strong>Frame mass.</strong> A heavy frame absorbs vibration instead
            of transmitting it, and it does not resonate. This is the main
            reason cheap treadmills get noisier over time while commercial ones
            do not — a light frame develops a hum once the bolts have settled and
            the panels start moving against each other. Weight is a proxy you
            can check on a spec sheet.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Cushioning depth.</strong> Cushioning exists to protect your
            joints, and it protects your neighbours by the same mechanism:
            energy spent deflecting a deck is energy that never reaches the
            floor. A firm deck is a loud deck downstairs. This is also why
            cushioning that hardens over time makes a machine noisier in month
            six than in week one.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Motor type and belt condition.</strong> A well-lubricated
            belt on true-tracking rollers is quiet; a dry belt squeaks and a
            mis-tracked one rubs the frame. Both are maintenance rather than
            purchase decisions, and both are common causes of a machine that was
            quiet becoming loud. Re-lubricate on the manufacturer's schedule and
            check tracking monthly.
          </p>
          <p>
            <strong>Console audio.</strong> Worth naming because it is the
            loudest thing on several machines and the easiest to fix. Interactive
            consoles ship with trainer audio at a high default volume, and on a
            couple of machines the startup chime alone is louder than the motor
            at walking pace. Turn it down before the first session.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. The 5 Quietest Treadmills We Tested</h2>
          <p>
            Decibel figures below are our 3 mph airborne measurements on a bare
            floor. The ranking, though, is by impact performance in the room
            below, which is why the order does not follow the decibel values.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Product cards -->
      <div class="not-prose">
