              </div>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <div id="mats" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Mats: The Cheapest Big Win</h2>
          <p>
            A dense rubber mat under the machine did more for our downstairs
            readings than any difference between the treadmills themselves. It
            costs a fraction of what a quieter machine costs, and if you are in
            an apartment it should be budgeted as part of the purchase rather
            than considered afterwards.
          </p>
          <p>
            Buy rubber, not foam. Foam gym tiles feel soft under a hand and
            compress completely under the weight of a treadmill plus a person,
            at which point they transmit almost everything. Dense rubber, 6mm or
            thicker, keeps working under load. Size it to the whole footprint of
            the machine including the rear feet, because a mat that stops short
            leaves two contact points coupling the frame directly to the floor —
            which defeats most of the benefit.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two refinements are worth the effort if the situation is difficult.
            Rubber anti-vibration pads under each foot, on top of the mat, add
            another layer of decoupling for a few pounds. And if your floor is a
            suspended timber one, adding a sheet of plywood between the mat and
            the machine spreads the load over more joists rather than
            concentrating it on one, which noticeably reduces flex.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="placement" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Placement and Floor Type</h2>
          <p>
            Where the machine sits within a room changes the outcome more than
            most people expect. A suspended floor flexes most at the centre of
            its span and least near a load-bearing wall, and flex is what
            converts footfall into sound below. Moving a treadmill three metres
            to sit against a supporting wall is free, takes ten minutes, and
            reliably reduces what carries downstairs.
          </p>
          <p>
            Floor type sets the ceiling on what you can achieve. A concrete slab
            is the best case: dense, stiff and disinclined to transmit
            low-frequency impact. A suspended timber floor is the difficult
            case, and it is what most apartments above the ground floor in older
            buildings have. If you are on a slab, a mat and sensible placement
            will almost certainly be enough. If you are on timber, expect to use
            every measure on this page.
          </p>
          <p>
            Two placement mistakes are worth avoiding outright. Do not push the
            machine hard against a shared wall — the frame couples directly to
            the structure and the wall becomes a diaphragm. And avoid siting it
            directly above a neighbour's bedroom when there is any alternative,
            because tolerance for rhythmic low-frequency noise is far lower at
            night than during the day. If space is so tight that placement
            cannot be chosen, a folding machine you can reposition between
            sessions is worth considering; our
            <a href="/folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">folding treadmill primer</a>
            covers which mechanisms survive that kind of daily handling.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="technique" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Free Fixes: Technique and Timing</h2>
          <p>
            How you walk changes what your neighbours hear. A heavy heel strike
            delivers a sharp impulse into the deck; a lighter, higher-cadence
            step spreads the same work over more, smaller impacts. Deliberately
            shortening your stride and increasing your cadence slightly is
            genuinely audible downstairs, and it costs nothing.
          </p>
          <p>
            Incline helps too, which is a useful coincidence. Walking uphill
            naturally shortens the stride and softens the foot strike, so an
            incline session at 2.5 mph is quieter downstairs than a flat session
            at 3.5 mph — while being the harder workout. If you want more effort
            and less noise at once, that is the trade to make. The structured
            version is in our
            <a href="/12-3-30-treadmill-workout/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">incline walking guide</a>.
          </p>
          <p>
            Finally, timing and one conversation. Most buildings define quiet
            hours between roughly 10pm and 7am, and impact noise is precisely
            what those rules exist for. Beyond compliance, ask your downstairs
            neighbour once, before you settle into a routine, and run the machine
            for ten minutes while they listen. Nearly every long-running dispute
            we hear about would have been avoided by that five-minute
            conversation.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="pads" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Are Walking Pads Any Quieter?</h2>
          <p>
            In the air, clearly yes. A walking pad at 2 mph uses a small motor
            and a slow belt, and it measured below every full-size machine in
            this test. If your concern is a partner in the same room or a
            colleague on a video call, a pad is the quieter device.
          </p>
          <p>
            Through the floor, the answer flips. A pad is thin because thinness
            is the point, and there is nowhere to fit a cushioning system, so
            footfall energy reaches the floor with much less absorbed along the
            way. On a bare floor, the pads we tested performed worse downstairs
            than the well-cushioned full-size machines here. With a rubber mat
            they improved substantially, which again points at the mat as the
            decisive purchase.
          </p>
          <p>
            So the answer depends on which neighbour you mean. Same room: the
            pad. Flat below: a heavy, cushioned treadmill on a mat, and the pad
            only if you also use one. The full comparison of the two formats,
            noise included, is in
            <a href="/walking-pad-vs-treadmill/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">walking pad vs. treadmill</a>,
            and the pads themselves are reviewed in our
            <a href="/under-desk-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">under-desk treadmill guide</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">9. Final Recommendations</h2>
          <p>
            If someone lives below you, buy the <strong>Sole F65</strong> and a
            6mm rubber mat. Heavy frame, deep cushioning, and the best result we
            measured in the room underneath. On a tighter budget the
            <strong>Sole F63</strong> gets most of the way there for several
            hundred dollars less, and the mat closes much of the remaining gap.
          </p>
          <p>
            If anyone in the household runs, the
            <strong>NordicTrack Commercial 1750</strong> handles the harder
            acoustic case better than anything else here, though it is noisier
            than the Soles at walking speed. The
            <strong>Life Fitness T3</strong> is the quietest machine in the air
            and worth buying for its durability rather than its silence.
          </p>
          <p>
            And whatever you buy: mat first, placement second, machine third.
            That is the order of effect we measured, and it is the reverse of the
            order most people shop in.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the quietest treadmill for an apartment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The Sole F65 was quietest in the room below, which is the
                measurement that decides whether neighbours complain. The Life
                Fitness T3 was marginally quieter in the air but costs nearly
                three times as much. If your budget stops below $1,000, the Sole
                F63 gets close to the F65 downstairs and is the sensible
                apartment buy.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do neighbours hear my treadmill even though it is quiet?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Because what they hear is impact, not motor noise. Each footfall
                sends energy through the deck, into the frame, into the floor
                and through the building structure, which carries low
                frequencies extremely well. Airborne noise stops at a wall;
                structure-borne noise does not. That is why a decibel figure
                measured beside the machine tells you almost nothing about what
                the flat below experiences.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do treadmill mats actually reduce noise?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A dense rubber mat does, and it is the single most effective
                thing you can buy. Aim for 6mm or thicker, sized to the whole
                footprint. Foam gym tiles are much less effective because they
                compress fully under load and then transmit through. In our
                testing, a proper rubber mat cut impact readings in the room
                below by more than any difference between machines.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Where should I put a treadmill in an apartment to reduce noise?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Near a load-bearing wall rather than in the middle of a room. A
                suspended floor flexes most at the centre of its span, and flex
                is what turns footfall into sound below. Avoid placing the
                machine directly above a neighbour's bedroom or living area if
                you have a choice, and never push it hard against a shared wall,
                which couples the frame directly to the structure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are walking pads quieter than treadmills?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                In the air, yes — smaller motors and lower speeds. Through the
                floor, often not, because a pad is thin by design and has almost
                no cushioning to absorb footfall before it reaches the
                structure. A heavy, well-cushioned treadmill on a rubber mat can
                be less disruptive downstairs than a thin pad on a bare floor.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What time of day is acceptable to use a treadmill in an apartment?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most building rules define quiet hours somewhere between 10pm
                and 7am, and impact noise is exactly the type those rules exist
                for. Beyond the rules, the practical answer is to ask your
                downstairs neighbour once, early, and run the machine for ten
                minutes while they listen. That conversation prevents almost
                every long-running dispute we hear about.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div class="mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Common Mistakes When Buying for a Quiet Home</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">All four of these come from optimising the wrong variable, and the first is by far the most expensive.</p>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Shopping by the advertised decibel figure</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">That number measures airborne noise beside the machine, and the complaint from downstairs is about impact through the structure. The two rankings genuinely invert: the quietest machine in the air in our test was not the quietest below it. Judge by frame mass and cushioning depth instead, which are the properties that absorb footfall before it reaches the floor.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Using foam tiles instead of a rubber mat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Foam feels soft to the hand and compresses completely under a treadmill plus a person, at which point it transmits nearly everything. Dense rubber at 6mm or thicker keeps working under load and made a bigger difference in our downstairs readings than any machine choice. Buy rubber, and size it to cover the rear feet too.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting the machine in the middle of the room</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A suspended floor flexes most at the centre of its span, and flex is what converts footfall into sound below. Moving the treadmill to sit near a load-bearing wall is free and reliably reduces transmission. Pushing it hard against a shared wall is the opposite mistake — that couples the frame straight into the structure.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Letting the belt go dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">A machine that was quiet on delivery and squeaks six months later almost always has a dry belt or one that has drifted off-centre and is rubbing the frame. Both are five-minute jobs described in the manual, and both are widely ignored. Re-lubricate on schedule and check tracking monthly, or the quiet machine you paid for will not stay quiet.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

